var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Package-guide","page":"Getting started and extension points","text":"L2ODLL takes a primal parametric JuMP model and builds, once, everything Dual Lagrangian Learning needs during training and inference:\n\nflowchart LR\n    M[\"primal JuMP model<br/>(parameters = MOI.Parameter)\"] --> D[\"decompose!(model)\"]\n    D --> C[\"DLLCache<br/>dual model (Dualization.jl)<br/>projection · completion · layer\"]\n    NN[\"proxy f(p)\"] -->|\"ŷ\"| C\n    P[\"parameters p\"] --> NN\n    P --> C\n    C -->|\"dual_objective\"| B[\"valid bound Φ(Π(ŷ), p)\"]\n    C -->|\"dual_objective_gradient\"| G[\"∇ŷ Φ\"]\n    C -->|\"dual_completion\"| Z[\"(y, z, w)\"]","title":"Package guide"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started and extension points","text":"julia> using JuMP, L2ODLL\n\njulia> model = Model();\n\njulia> @variable(model, 0 <= x[1:2] <= 1);\n\njulia> @variable(model, d in MOI.Parameter(1.0));    # parameter: the demand\n\njulia> @constraint(model, balance, x[1] + x[2] == d);\n\njulia> @objective(model, Min, x[1] + 3x[2]);\n\njulia> cache = decompose!(model);                    # auto-detects BoundDecomposition\n\njulia> cache.decomposition isa BoundDecomposition\ntrue\n\njulia> get_y(model)                                  # predicted: the balance constraint\n1-element Vector{ConstraintRef}:\n balance : x[1] + x[2] - d = 0\n\njulia> y_shape(model)\n1-element Vector{Int64}:\n 1\n\njulia> dual_objective(model, [[2.0]], [1.5])          # Φ for y = 2 at d = 1.5\n2.0\n\njulia> dual_objective(model, [[3.0]], [1.5])          # the optimal price: tight bound\n2.5\n\nFor d = 15 the optimum is x = (1 05) with cost 25; every y gives a lower bound, and the marginal price y = 3 gives the optimum.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Concepts","page":"Getting started and extension points","text":"Parameters are the inputs of the proxy, in the order filter(is_parameter, all_variables(model)).\ny is a vector of vectors: one block per constraint in get_y, of length y_shape (1 for scalar constraints, the cone dimension for vector constraints). L2ODLL.flatten_y and L2ODLL.unflatten_y convert to and from a flat vector (the proxy output).\nSigns follow JuMP.dual: JuMP.dual.(get_y(model)) after solving is a valid (optimal) prediction.\nBatches use matrices with the batch as the last dimension: dual_objective(model, Y, P) with Y of size sum(y_shape(model)) × B and P of size num_parameters × B; dual_objective_gradient and dual_objective_and_gradient have the same matrix forms.\nCompletion: dual_completion returns the projected y, the completed z (one block per constraint in get_z) and the quadratic slacks w.\nDifferentiation uses DifferentiationInterface.jl: pass any ad_type (default AutoForwardDiff()). The closed-form layers are plain array code, so reverse-mode backends such as Zygote also work.","title":"Concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Decompositions","page":"Getting started and extension points","text":"decomposition when predicted y completed completion\nBoundDecomposition every variable has finite bounds, objective affine in x all constraints except bounds bound duals z_l z_u closed form (Theory eq. 4)\nConvexQP strictly convex objective (Hessian Q succ 0; prec 0 for Max) all constraints quadratic slacks w closed form (Theory eq. 6)\nGenericDecomposition any; built by hand the constraints you choose the others a solver (optimizer)\n\nSee Decompositions and Decomposition detection for the derivations.","title":"Decompositions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Extension-points","page":"Getting started and extension points","text":"","title":"Extension points"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Custom-decompositions","page":"Getting started and extension points","text":"Choose the predicted constraints yourself and let a solver complete the rest:\n\nimport ParametricOptInterface as POI, HiGHS\nd = GenericDecomposition(model, [balance])               # predict only `balance`\ndecompose!(model, d; optimizer = () -> POI.Optimizer(HiGHS.Optimizer()))\n\nThe completion problem (Theory eq. 3) is solved with the given optimizer each call; the optimizer must support parameters (e.g. through ParametricOptInterface). The completion may be infeasible for an arbitrary split (Theory, hypothesis of Theorem 1); the call then throws.\n\nA new decomposition type subtypes AbstractDecomposition with fields p_ref and y_ref, implements completed_constraints (or has a z_ref field) and, to take part in auto-detection, can_decompose. A closed form is supplied through the builders below.","title":"Custom decompositions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Projection-and-completion-options","page":"Getting started and extension points","text":"decompose!(model; projection = :radial) uses the radial projection of the DLL paper for second-order cones (eq. 26, closed form eq. 39), which keeps a nonzero gradient where the Euclidean projection is identically zero. decompose!(model; completion = :log, μ = 0.1) (bounded decomposition) uses the log-barrier completion (Theory eq. 5). Both keep the bounds valid and work with the batched layer; DLLMethod(projection = :radial) and DLLMethod(completion = :log, μ = 0.1) forward them.","title":"Projection and completion options"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Custom-projections","page":"Getting started and extension points","text":"Any map into the dual cone keeps the bound valid, e.g. the radial projection of DLL (eq. 26). Pass a function from y (vector of vectors) to projected y:\n\ndecompose!(model, BoundDecomposition(model); proj_fn = y -> my_projection.(y))\n\nL2ODLL.make_jump_proj_fn builds a solver-based projection for cones without a closed-form projection.","title":"Custom projections"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Custom-layers-and-completions","page":"Getting started and extension points","text":"dll_layer_builder(decomposition, proj_fn, dual_model) must return (y_pred, param_value) -> Φ, and completion_builder (same arguments) must return (y_pred, param_value) -> (y = ..., z = ..., w = ...). The built-in builders are L2ODLL.bounded_builder, L2ODLL.convex_qp_builder, L2ODLL.jump_builder and their *_completion_builder counterparts, e.g. the smoothed log-barrier completion:\n\ndecompose!(model, BoundDecomposition(model);\n    dll_layer_builder = (d, proj, dm) -> L2ODLL.bounded_builder(d, proj, dm; completion = :log, μ = 0.1),\n    completion_builder = (d, proj, dm) -> L2ODLL.bounded_completion_builder(d, proj, dm; completion = :log, μ = 0.1))","title":"Custom layers and completions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#As-an-L2OCore-method","page":"Getting started and extension points","text":"DLLMethod plugs L2ODLL into the method-agnostic LearningToOptimize pipeline; see L2OCore interface.\n\n","title":"As an L2OCore method"},{"category":"section","location":"background/decomposition_detection/#Decomposition-detection","page":"Decomposition detection","text":"warning: Warning\nThe decomposition detection is very basic, based on minimizing the number of predicted dual variables. In some cases, it may actually be preferred to to predict more dual variables, if they are for some reason easier to learn. In that case, users should manually specify the decomposition.\n\nL2ODLL automatically detects the decomposition to use based on the model. The current detection logic is as follows:\n\nIf all variables have finite upper and lower bounds and the objective is affine in the decision variables, use the bounded decomposition.\nIf the objective is strictly convex (positive definite Hessian for Min, negative definite for Max), use the convex QP decomposition.\nOtherwise, no closed form is available: build a GenericDecomposition by hand and pass an optimizer to decompose!.\n\nThe order of preference is to maximize the number of completed dual variables among the closed-form solutions. The analysis below shows that for a convex QP with bounds on every variable, the bounded decomposition would complete more dual variables than the convex QP decomposition.\n\nnote: Current behavior\nThe bounded decomposition with a quadratic objective (predicting y and w, completing z_l z_u) is not implemented yet: the network would have to predict the quadratic slack w, which is not the dual of a constraint. Such models are therefore detected as ConvexQP (when every variable has a quadratic term), and decompose!(model, BoundDecomposition(model)) raises an error for them.","title":"Decomposition detection"},{"category":"section","location":"background/decomposition_detection/#Bounded-with-quadratic-objective","page":"Decomposition detection","text":"In the case where bounded decomposition is used with a model that has a quadratic objective, L2ODLL prefers the bounded decomposition, leading to the following models.\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  x^top Q x + c^top x\n\n textst  Ax + b in mathcalC\n\n  l leq x leq u\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_wyz_lz_u  - b^top y - w^top Q w + l^top z_l + u^top z_u\n\n textst  A^top y - 2Qw + z_l + z_u = c\n\n  y in mathcalC^* w in mathbbR^n z_l in mathbbR_+^n z_u in mathbbR_-^n\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThen, the completion model is:\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_z_lz_u  + l^top z_l + u^top z_u - b^top y - w^top Q w\n\n textst  z_l + z_u = c - A^top y + 2Qw   \n\n  z_l in mathbbR_+^n z_u in mathbbR_-^n\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  (c-A^top y + 2Qw)^top x - b^top y - w^top Q w\n\n  l leq x leq u\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThis model admits a closed form solution, z_l = c-A^top y+2Qw^+ and z_u = -c-A^top y+2Qw^-. Furthermore, the x that defines the (sub-)gradient is given element-wise by l if c-A^top y+2Qw  0, u if c-A^top y+2Qw  0, and xinlu otherwise. Note that the gradient of the objective with respect to w is nabla_w = -(Q+Q^top)w - 2Q^top x.\n\nThis completes 2n dual variables, leaving the neural network to predict m+n dual variables.\n\nConsider the convex QP decomposition in this case:\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  x^top Q x + c^top x\n\n textst  Ax + b in mathcalC\n\n  l leq x leq u\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_wyz_lz_u  - b^top y - w^top Q w + l^top z_l + u^top z_u\n\n textst  A^top y - 2Qw + z_l + z_u = c\n\n  y in mathcalC^* w in mathbbR^n z_l in mathbbR_+^n z_u in mathbbR_-^n\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThen, the completion model is:\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_w  - w^top Q w - b^top y + l^top z_l + u^top z_u \n\n textst  Qw = frac12(A^top y + z_l + z_u - c)   \n\n  w in mathbbR^n\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  x^top Q x + (c-A^top y - z_l - z_u)^top x - b^top y + l^top z_l + u^top z_u\n\n  x in mathbbR^n\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThis model admits a closed form solution, w = frac12Q^-1(A^top y + z_l + z_u - c). Furthermore, the closed form dual solution in this case is x=w.\n\nThis completes n dual variables, leaving the neural network to predict m+2n dual variables.\n\nNaturally, one may consider using both decompositions, i.e. to predict only the y variables and to recover w, z_l, and z_u. Let us consider this case:\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  x^top Q x + c^top x\n\n textst  Ax + b in mathcalC\n\n  l leq x leq u\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_wyz_lz_u  - b^top y - w^top Q w + l^top z_l + u^top z_u\n\n textst  A^top y - 2Qw + z_l + z_u = c\n\n  y in mathcalC^* w in mathbbR^n z_l in mathbbR_+^n z_u in mathbbR_-^n\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThen, the completion model is:\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_wz_lz_u  - w^top Q w + l^top z_l + u^top z_u - b^top y\n\n textst  -2Qw + z_l + z_u = c - A^top y\n\n  w in mathbbR^n z_l in mathbbR_+^n z_u in mathbbR_-^n\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  x^top Q x + (c-A^top y)^top x - b^top y\n\n  l leq x leq u\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThis is an n-dimensional box-constrained convex QP, for which there is no closed form solution. Note that by using a custom generic decomposition, L2ODLL can still be used to set up this problem and solve it using a JuMP-compatible QP solver.\n\n","title":"Bounded with quadratic objective"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/l2ocore/#L2OCore-interface-and-design-notes","page":"L2OCore interface and design notes","text":"L2OCore (in lib/L2OCore of LearningToOptimize.jl) defines a small, method-agnostic pipeline shared by the Learning-to-Optimize packages: a ParametricProblem, methods that declare which problem classes they supports, and the steps decompose → generate_data → loss → complete → evaluate. L2ODLL implements it as a dual method.\n\nflowchart LR\n    PP[\"ParametricProblem(model)\"] --> PIPE[\"L2OPipeline(problem, DLLMethod())\"]\n    PIPE -->|\"decompose\"| DEC[\"DLLDecomposition<br/>(L2ODLL.decompose! + index map)\"]\n    X[\"inputs P (params × B)\"] --> NN[\"proxy\"]\n    NN -->|\"output (sum(y_shape) × B)\"| COMP[\"complete → Prediction(dual)\"]\n    NN --> LOSS[\"loss = ∓ mean Φ\"]\n    COMP --> EV[\"evaluate: dual bound, dual infeasibility\"]\n\nL2OCore function DLL implementation\nsupports LinearConic, ConvexQuadratic\ndecompose decompose! (auto-detected, or DLLMethod(decomposition = BoundDecomposition))\noutput_dimension sum(y_shape(model))\ncomplete projection + completion; full dual in dual_layout order with JuMP.dual signs\nloss self-supervised (Theory eq. 7): -textmeanPhi (min) / +textmeanPhi (max)\ngenerate_data inputs only; with optimizer, also optimal objectives and duals (for gaps)\n\nimport L2OCore, L2ODLL, Clarabel\nproblem = L2OCore.ParametricProblem(model)\npipe = L2OCore.L2OPipeline(problem, L2ODLL.DLLMethod())\ndata = L2OCore.generate_data(pipe; sampler = L2OCore.BoxSampler(lo, hi), n = 100,\n                             optimizer = Clarabel.Optimizer)       # solver optima for gaps\nY = proxy(data.inputs)                                              # sum(y_shape) × 100\nℓ = L2OCore.loss(pipe, data.inputs, Y)                              # minimize this\npred = L2OCore.complete(pipe, data.inputs, Y)\nreport = L2OCore.evaluate(problem, data.inputs, pred)             # dual bound, dual infeasibility\ngap = (data.objective .- report.dual_bound) ./ max.(1, abs.(data.objective))\n\nThe economic dispatch example runs this end to end.","title":"L2OCore interface and design notes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/l2ocore/#Why-a-package-extension","page":"L2OCore interface and design notes","text":"L2OCore adds no new transitive dependencies (it uses JuMP, MathOptInterface and standard libraries, all already dependencies of L2ODLL), so weight is not the concern. It is, however, not registered yet and is installed from a branch. Making it a hard dependency would make every installation and every CI run of L2ODLL depend on that branch. As a weak dependency:\n\nusing L2ODLL works and is tested without L2OCore;\nimport L2OCore loads the extension L2ODLLL2OCoreExt, which defines the method type; DLLMethod is its constructor (calling it without L2OCore loaded prints a hint);\nonce L2OCore is registered, the [sources] entry and the CI step for Julia 1.10 can be dropped; the extension can stay.","title":"Why a package extension"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/l2ocore/#Automatic-differentiation","page":"L2OCore interface and design notes","text":"DLL's loss needs only first-order derivatives of Phi with respect to the proxy output (then the chain rule through the proxy), so it does not need nested AD.\n\nbackend through dual_objective / L2OCore.loss tested against\nForwardDiff yes (default ad_type) central finite differences\nZygote yes ForwardDiff, to 10^-10\nFiniteDifferences / other DI backends yes, via ad_type —\nsolver-based layers (jump_builder) no AD; gradients would need the dual of the completion (issue #5) —\n\nConsequently any Julia ML framework whose training loop uses Zygote (Flux, Lux) can train a DLL proxy: the proxy is just a callable mapping parameters to the sum(y_shape) × B output.","title":"Automatic differentiation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/l2ocore/#GPU-and-batching","page":"L2OCore interface and design notes","text":"For the closed-form decompositions, decompose! also builds a batched layer (batched_layer). It evaluates a whole batch (one instance per column) with matrix products and broadcasts only, so it\n\nis used automatically by the matrix forms dual_objective(model, Y, P) and dual_objective_and_gradient(model, Y, P) and by the L2OCore complete/loss;\ndifferentiates with Zygote (one pullback for the batch) and with ForwardDiff (one pushforward per row of Y, independent of the batch size);\nis written for accelerator arrays: no scalar indexing, no mutation.\n\nusing Adapt, CUDA                       # or any GPU array package\nlayer = Adapt.adapt(CuArray, L2ODLL.densify(batched_layer(model)))\nY, P = CuArray(Y), CuArray(P)           # sum(y_shape) × B, num_parameters × B\nbounds = layer(Y, P)                    # CuVector of valid bounds\n∇ = Zygote.gradient(Y -> sum(layer(Y, P)), Y)[1]\nc = batched_completion(layer, Y, P)     # (y, z, w) as device matrices\n\nThe test suite runs this path with JLArrays (a reference GPU array type that forbids scalar indexing) and compares values, Zygote gradients and completions with the CPU ones. CI has no GPU, so the CUDA snippet above is not run there. Projections onto cones other than scalar sets, orthants and second-order cones fall back to a per-column CPU projection. ExaModels and BatchNLPKernels-based evaluation in L2OCore is complementary: it evaluates the primal problem, while this layer evaluates the dual bound.\n\n","title":"GPU and batching"},{"category":"section","location":"background/decompositions/#Decompositions","page":"Decompositions","text":"In DLL, the primal constraints (dual variables) are decomposed into a predicted set and a completed set. Consider the primal-dual pair:\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  c^top x\n\n textst  Ax + b in mathcalC\n\n  x in mathbbR^n\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_y  - b^top y\n\n textst  A^top y = c\n\n  y in mathcalC^*\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nAfter the decomposition, we have:\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  c^top x\n\n textst  Ax + b in mathcalC\n\n phantomtextst  Hx + h in mathcalK\n\n  x in mathbbR^n\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_y  - b^top y\n\n textst  A^top y + H^top z = c\n\n  y in mathcalC^* z in mathcalK^*\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThen, the completion model is:\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_z  - h^top z - b^top y\n\n textst  H^top z = c - A^top y\n\n  z in mathcalK^*\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  (c-A^top y)^top x - b^top y\n\n phantomtextst  Hx + h in mathcalK\n\n  x in mathbbR^n\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nTo train the neural network, we need the gradient of the optimal value with respect to the predicted y. This is nabla_y = -b-Ax where x is the optimal dual solution corresponding to the affine constraints in the completion model. In the special cases below, we specify just the expression for x in this formula.","title":"Decompositions"},{"category":"section","location":"background/decompositions/#Bounded-Decomposition","page":"Decompositions","text":"When all primal variables have finite upper and lower bounds, a natural way to decompose the constraints is to have z correspond to the bound constraints, and y correspond to the main constraints, i.e.\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  c^top x\n\n textst  Ax + b in mathcalC\n\n  l leq x leq u\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_yz_lz_u  - b^top y + l^top z_l + u^top z_u\n\n textst  A^top y + z_l + z_u = c\n\n  y in mathcalC^* z_l in mathbbR_+^n z_u in mathbbR_-^n\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThen, the completion model is:\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_z_lz_u  + l^top z_l + u^top z_u - b^top y\n\n textst  z_l + z_u = c - A^top y\n\n  z_l in mathbbR_+^n z_u in mathbbR_-^n\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  (c-A^top y)^top x - b^top y\n\n  l leq x leq u\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThis model admits a closed form solution, z_l = c-A^top y^+ and z_u = -c-A^top y^-. Furthermore, the x that defines the (sub-)gradient is given element-wise by l if c-A^top y  0, u if c-A^top y  0, and xinlu otherwise.","title":"Bounded Decomposition"},{"category":"section","location":"background/decompositions/#(Strictly)-Convex-QP","page":"Decompositions","text":"In the convex QP case, the primal has a strictly convex quadratic objective function, i.e. Qsucc 0. In that case it is natural to use the main constraints as the predicted set and to complete the quadratic slack dual variables.\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  x^top Q x + c^top x\n\n textst  Ax + b in mathcalC\n\n  x in mathbbR^n\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_y  - b^top y - z^top Q z\n\n textst  A^top y - 2Qz = c\n\n  y in mathcalC^* z in mathbbR^n\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThen, the completion model is:\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_z  - z^top Q z - b^top y\n\n textst  Q z = frac12(A^top y - c)\n\n  z in mathbbR^n\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  x^top Q x + (c-A^top y)^top x - b^top y\n\n  x in mathbbR^n\nendaligned\nendequation\n\nThis model admits a closed form solution, z = frac12Q^-1(A^top y - c), the minimizer of the Lagrangian. Furthermore, the closed form dual solution in this case is x=z (with this sign convention for z, which is the one Dualization.jl uses; checked by the QuadSlackRecoversPrimal test).\n\n","title":"(Strictly) Convex QP"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Theory","page":"Dual Lagrangian Learning","text":"This page states the optimization problems behind L2ODLL, the dual completions it computes in closed form, and their gradients. References:\n\n[DLL] M. Tanneau and P. Van Hentenryck, Dual Lagrangian Learning for Conic Optimization, NeurIPS 2024, arXiv:2402.03086.\n[DIPOL] M. Klamkin, M. Tanneau and P. Van Hentenryck, Dual Interior Point Optimization Learning, arXiv:2402.02596.\n\nEquation numbers such as \"(DLL 9)\" refer to the arXiv versions of these papers.","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Notation","page":"Dual Lagrangian Learning","text":"symbol meaning\np in mathbbR^d parameters (JuMP MOI.Parameter variables); the input of the proxy\nx in mathbbR^n primal decision variables\nmathcalC mathcalK closed convex cones (products of MOI sets: mathbbR_+, 0, second-order, PSD, …)\nmathcalC^* dual cone, y  y^top s ge 0 forall s in mathcalC (DLL 2)\ny predicted dual variables (one block per constraint in get_y)\nz completed dual variables (one block per constraint in get_z)\nw quadratic slack variables of the dual (convex quadratic objectives)\nPi_mathcalC^* Euclidean projection onto mathcalC^* (DLL 1)\nlvert vrvert^+ = max(v 0), lvert vrvert^- = max(-v 0) elementwise\n\nSign convention. L2ODLL follows MathOptInterface: a constraint is written as \"function in set\", Ax + b in mathcalC, and its dual is y in mathcalC^* with the signs returned by JuMP.dual. The papers write Ax succeq_mathcalK b; the two forms coincide with b mapsto -b. Maximization problems are handled by Dualization.jl, so the predicted y always has JuMP.dual signs.","title":"Notation"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Parametric-primal–dual-pair","page":"Dual Lagrangian Learning","text":"For fixed parameters p consider the conic problem and its dual (DLL 4–5):\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  c(p)^top x\n\n textst  Ax + b(p) in mathcalC\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_y  - b(p)^top y\n\n textst  A^top y = c(p)\n\n  y in mathcalC^*\nendaligned\ntag1\nendequation\n\nParametrization. The parameters enter affinely in the data b(p) and c(p) (constraint constants and linear objective coefficients, e.g. @objective(m, Max, μ' * x) with parameters μ). The constraint matrix A and the quadratic objective Q do not depend on p; a parameter multiplying a variable in a constraint is rejected with an error.\n\nWeak duality. For every p and every dual-feasible y (A^top y = c(p), y in mathcalC^*), -b(p)^top y le the optimal value of the primal. No convexity of the parameter dependence and no constraint qualification is needed. Strong duality (equality at the optimum) holds under Slater's condition.","title":"Parametric primal–dual pair"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Decomposition-and-completion","page":"Dual Lagrangian Learning","text":"Split the constraints into a predicted block (cone mathcalC, duals y) and a completed block Hx + h(p) in mathcalK (duals z) (DLL 6–7):\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\n maxnolimits_yz  - b(p)^top y - h(p)^top z\n\n textst  A^top y + H^top z = c(p)\n\n  y in mathcalC^* z in mathcalK^*\nendaligned\ntag2\nendequation\n\nGiven a prediction hat y, DLL projects it, bar y = Pi_mathcalC^*(hat y), and completes z by solving (DLL 9), whose dual is (DLL 8):\n\nbeginequation\nbeginaligned\nPhi(bar y p) =  maxnolimits_z  - h(p)^top z - b(p)^top bar y\n\n textst  H^top z = c(p) - A^top bar y\n\n  z in mathcalK^*\nendaligned\nquadquadquadquad\nbeginaligned\n minnolimits_x  (c(p) - A^top bar y)^top x - b(p)^top bar y\n\n textst  Hx + h(p) in mathcalK\nendaligned\ntag3\nendequation\n\ndual_objective returns Phi(bar y p); dual_completion returns (bar y z w).\n\nTheorem (DLL Theorem 1, dual conic completion). Assume that for every ``\\bar y \\in \\mathcal{C}^the right-hand problem in (3) is bounded and strictly feasible Then the left-hand problem in (3) has an optimalz and(\\bar y, z)`` is feasible for (2).*\n\nTheorem (DLL Theorem 2, optimal completion). Under the same assumptions, Phi(bar y p) is a valid bound on the primal optimum, and it is the best bound among all dual-feasible points whose y-block equals bar y.\n\nIn particular Phi(y^star p) equals the optimal value when y^star is the y-block of an optimal dual solution.","title":"Decomposition and completion"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Closed-form-completions","page":"Dual Lagrangian Learning","text":"The hypothesis of Theorem 1 holds, and (3) has a closed form, in two structured cases that L2ODLL detects automatically (decomposition detection).\n\nBounded variables (DLL Example 1, eqs. 11–18; BoundDecomposition). If l le x le u with finite l  u and the objective is affine in x, take the bounds as the completed block. With r = c(p) - A^top bar y,\n\nz_l = lvert rrvert^+ qquad z_u = -lvert rrvert^-\nqquad\nPhi(bar y p) = -b(p)^top bar y + l^top z_l + u^top z_u \ntag4\n\n(L2ODLL keeps z_u le 0, the MOI sign of the dual of x le u; DLL writes z^u = lvert rrvert^- ge 0 with a minus sign in the objective.)\n\nLog-barrier completion (DIPOL Theorem 2, eq. 7). Replacing the completion objective by -h^top z + mu sum_j (ln z_lj + ln(-z_uj)) gives, with v = mu  (u - l) and omega = r2,\n\nz_l = v + omega + sqrtv^2 + omega^2 qquad\nz_u = -v + omega - sqrtv^2 + omega^2\ntag5\n\nIt still satisfies z_l + z_u = r with z_l  0  z_u, so the bound (4) evaluated at (5) is valid, and it tends to (4) as mu to 0; it is smooth in bar y. It is available as L2ODLL.LogBoundedCompletion in L2ODLL.bounded_builder(...; completion = :log, μ).\n\nStrictly convex quadratic objective (DLL Example 3, eqs. 21–23; ConvexQP). For min tfrac12 x^top Q x + c(p)^top x with Q succ 0 the dual has a free block w with stationarity A^top y - Q w = c(p) (the sign convention of Dualization.jl). Predicting all constraint duals and completing w,\n\nw = Q^-1big(A^top bar y - c(p)big) qquad\nPhi(bar y p) = -b(p)^top bar y - tfrac12 w^top Q w \ntag6\n\nw is the minimizer of the Lagrangian, x(bar y); at an optimal y^star it is the (unique) primal optimum. (The code reads the coefficient matrix of w from the dual written by Dualization.jl, so the tfrac12 convention of the model does not matter.)","title":"Closed-form completions"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Projection","page":"Dual Lagrangian Learning","text":"The projection Pi_mathcalC^* is computed per constraint with MathOptSetDistances.jl (Euclidean projection, DLL eq. 1): max(y, 0) for mathbbR_+, the identity for equalities, the closed-form projection for second-order cones, eigenvalue clipping for PSD cones. DLL also proposes a radial projection (DLL 26) that avoids zero gradients on the polar cone; any map into mathcalC^* keeps the bound valid, and a custom one can be passed as proj_fn to decompose! (see the package guide).","title":"Projection"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Gradient-of-the-completed-dual-objective","page":"Dual Lagrangian Learning","text":"Training needs nabla_hat yPhi(Pi_mathcalC^*(hat y) p).\n\nTheorem (DIPOL Theorem 3). For the bounded completion (4)–(5) with mu ge 0, Phi is concave in bar y and -b(p) - Ahat x is a supergradient, where hat x is an optimal dual solution of the equality constraints in (3) (the right-hand problem). For mu  0 it is differentiable.\n\nTheorem (DIPOL Theorem 4). For mu = 0, hat x_j = l_j if r_j  0, hat x_j = u_j if r_j  0 and any hat x_j in l_j u_j if r_j = 0.\n\nIn the convention of the papers (Ax ge b) this reads b - Ahat x. The same argument (partial Lagrangian of (3)) gives -b(p) - Ahat x for the generic completion, with hat x the dual of the equality constraints of the completion problem (issue #5). When the network also predicts a quadratic slack w (bounded decomposition with a quadratic objective, not implemented yet) its gradient with respect to w is Q(hat x - w) for the objective tfrac12 x^top Q x (see decomposition detection).\n\nL2ODLL differentiates the closed forms (4) and (6) with automatic differentiation (ForwardDiff by default via DifferentiationInterface; Zygote also works), which yields the same (super)gradient almost everywhere.","title":"Gradient of the completed dual objective"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Training-problem","page":"Dual Lagrangian Learning","text":"The proxy hat y = f_theta(p) is trained self-supervised by maximizing the expected bound (DLL 27a; DIPOL 3):\n\nmax_theta mathbbE_pbigPhi(Pi_mathcalC^*(f_theta(p)) p)big\ntag7\n\nNo optimal solutions are needed. For a maximization primal the dual is a minimization and (7) becomes a minimization. In the L2OCore interface the loss is always minimized, so it is -textmeanPhi for minimization problems and +textmeanPhi for maximization problems.","title":"Training problem"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Deviations-from-the-papers","page":"Dual Lagrangian Learning","text":"Signs. MOI convention throughout (see above); both forms define the same bound.\nProjection. Euclidean projection by default; DLL's radial projection is available for second-order cones with projection = :radial.\nBounded decomposition with a quadratic objective. The background pages describe predicting (y w) and completing the bounds; this is not implemented yet. Such models are detected as ConvexQP (if the objective is strictly convex), and requesting BoundDecomposition explicitly raises an error.\nLog-barrier layer. With completion = :log, the layer returns the plain dual objective (4) evaluated at the smoothed completion (5): a valid bound, smooth in bar y. DIPOL trains with the barrier-augmented objective (DIPOL eq. 4), whose supergradient is -b - A x_mu (Theorems 3, 5); the plain objective has a different gradient. The barrier-augmented value can be built from L2ODLL.complete_zlzu, which returns (z_l z_u) of (5).\nInfeasible generic completions. Only the two closed forms satisfy the hypothesis of Theorem 1 by construction. A GenericDecomposition completion may be infeasible (issue #6); the JuMP layer then throws instead of returning a bound.\n\n","title":"Deviations from the papers"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/economic_dispatch/#Economic-dispatch:-training-a-dual-proxy","page":"Economic dispatch: training a proxy","text":"Generators g = 1 dots G with linear costs c_g and capacities overlinep_g must meet a demand d and keep a spinning reserve r:\n\nmin_p c^top p quadtextstquad mathbf1^top p = dquad\nmathbf1^top(overlinep - p) ge rquad 0 le p le overlinep\n\n(d r) are the parameters. The dual variables of the two coupling constraints are the energy and reserve prices. We train a small linear proxy hat y = W d r + w_0 to predict them, self-supervised: the loss is the (negated) completed dual objective (eq. (27a) of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck, 2024), so no optimal solutions are needed for training, and every prediction along the way is a valid lower bound on the dispatch cost.\n\nusing JuMP, Random, Statistics\nusing HiGHS: HiGHS\nusing L2ODLL\n\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nG = 10\ncost = sort(10 .+ 40 .* rand(rng, G))\npmax = 50 .+ 100 .* rand(rng, G)\n\nmodel = Model()\n@variable(model, 0 <= p[g=1:G] <= pmax[g])\n@variable(model, d in MOI.Parameter(500.0))\n@variable(model, r in MOI.Parameter(50.0))\n@constraint(model, balance, sum(p) == d)\n@constraint(model, reserve, sum(pmax[g] - p[g] for g in 1:G) >= r)\n@objective(model, Min, cost' * p)\ndecompose!(model)\ny_shape(model)\n2-element Vector{Int64}:\n 1\n 1\n\nParameters: demand between 30% and 80% of capacity, reserve 5–10% of demand.\n\nfunction sample_params(rng, n)\n    d = (0.3 .+ 0.5 .* rand(rng, n)) .* sum(pmax)\n    return permutedims(hcat(d, (0.05 .+ 0.05 .* rand(rng, n)) .* d))\nend\nPtrain = sample_params(rng, 256)\nPtest = sample_params(rng, 64)\n2×64 Matrix{Float64}:\n 361.661  680.165   443.273   465.64    …  737.816   429.854   494.128\n  30.861   51.3786   33.6423   43.1952      56.1795   41.0019   32.5513\n\nReference optima (only to report the gap on the test set):\n\nset_optimizer(model, HiGHS.Optimizer);\nset_silent(model);\nfunction optimum(model, pv)\n    set_parameter_value(d, pv[1])\n    set_parameter_value(r, pv[2])\n    optimize!(model)\n    return objective_value(model)\nend\nopt_test = [optimum(model, Ptest[:, k]) for k in axes(Ptest, 2)]\n64-element Vector{Float64}:\n  4367.571391892745\n 12737.316410494572\n  5557.264252023053\n  5887.470040166691\n  7686.523831623461\n 11066.776414535278\n 11090.102839964762\n 17277.073617693764\n 16946.490658941395\n  7957.8622770390875\n     ⋮\n 12338.733732717083\n  6108.121988610666\n 10734.736507619951\n  6694.5167580373945\n 13270.132574061034\n  8938.169256873145\n 14909.277271376659\n  5359.149606283206\n  6453.309624540778\n\nA linear proxy on scaled inputs and plain gradient ascent on the mean bound. dual_objective_and_gradient(model, Y, P) returns the bound and its gradient with respect to the prediction for every column.\n\nscale = sum(pmax)\nW = zeros(2, 2);\nw0 = zeros(2);\nproxy(W, w0, P) = W * (P ./ scale) .+ w0\ngap(W, w0) = mean((opt_test .- dual_objective(model, proxy(W, w0, Ptest), Ptest)) ./ opt_test)\n\nhistory = Float64[]\nfor epoch in 1:300\n    Y = proxy(W, w0, Ptrain)\n    _, Gy = dual_objective_and_gradient(model, Y, Ptrain)    # ∂Φ/∂ŷ, 2 × 256\n    global W += 0.5 .* Gy * (Ptrain ./ scale)' ./ size(Ptrain, 2) ./ scale\n    global w0 += 0.5 .* vec(mean(Gy; dims=2)) ./ scale\n    push!(history, gap(W, w0))\nend\nhistory[[1, 10, 100, 300]]\n4-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.9768395057776958\n 0.768395057776959\n 0.14077266846725633\n 0.09785415413027916\n\nThe relative gap on held-out instances decreases from about 100% to about 10%. The optimal prices are piecewise constant in (d r) (they jump at the merit order), which a linear proxy cannot represent; a neural network trained with the same loss does better. Whatever the proxy, every bound is valid:\n\nbounds = dual_objective(model, proxy(W, w0, Ptest), Ptest)\nall(bounds .<= opt_test .+ 1e-6)\ntrue","title":"Economic dispatch: training a dual proxy"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/economic_dispatch/#The-same-through-L2OCore","page":"Economic dispatch: training a proxy","text":"With import L2OCore the method plugs into the shared LearningToOptimize pipeline. Its evaluate recomputes the Lagrangian dual function of the completed duals independently, together with their dual infeasibility:\n\nusing L2OCore: L2OCore\nproblem = L2OCore.ParametricProblem(model)\npipe = L2OCore.L2OPipeline(problem, DLLMethod())\npred = L2OCore.complete(pipe, Ptest, proxy(W, w0, Ptest))\nreport = L2OCore.evaluate(problem, Ptest, pred)\nmaximum(report.dual_infeasibility), maximum(abs.(report.dual_bound .- bounds))\n(0.0, 1.8189894035458565e-12)\n\nand its loss is the negated mean bound used above:\n\nL2OCore.loss(pipe, Ptest, proxy(W, w0, Ptest)) ≈ -mean(bounds)\ntrue\n\n","title":"The same through L2OCore"},{"category":"section","location":"public/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"public/#Building-the-DLL-layers","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Building the DLL layers"},{"category":"section","location":"public/#Evaluating-predictions","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Evaluating predictions"},{"category":"section","location":"public/#Decompositions","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Decompositions"},{"category":"section","location":"public/#Layer-builders-and-projections","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Layer builders and projections"},{"category":"section","location":"public/#Batched-layers","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Batched layers"},{"category":"section","location":"public/#L2OCore-interface","page":"API reference","text":"Defined in the package extension L2ODLLL2OCoreExt (load with import L2OCore).\n\n","title":"L2OCore interface"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.decompose!","page":"API reference","text":"decompose!(model::JuMP.Model; kwargs...)\n\nDetect the best decomposition and build the DLL functions. Keyword arguments are those of decompose!(model, decomposition; ...).\n\n\n\n\n\ndecompose!(model::JuMP.Model, decomposition::AbstractDecomposition;\n    optimizer = nothing, proj_fn = nothing, projection = :euclidean,\n    completion = :exact, μ = 1.0, dll_layer_builder = nothing, completion_builder = nothing)\n\nBuild the DLL functions using the given decomposition.\n\nprojection: :euclidean (default) or :radial (eq. 26 of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck, 2024, for second-order cones; see BatchedProjection). Ignored when a custom proj_fn is given.\ncompletion, μ (BoundDecomposition only): :exact (Example 1 of the DLL paper) or :log, the log-barrier completion with parameter μ > 0 (eq. 7 of Klamkin, Tanneau and Van Hentenryck); both give valid bounds.\noptimizer: solver for decompositions without a closed form.\ndll_layer_builder, completion_builder: custom layers (see build_cache).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.decompose!"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.build_cache","page":"API reference","text":"build_cache(model::JuMP.Model, decomposition::AbstractDecomposition;\n    optimizer=nothing, proj_fn=nothing, dll_layer_builder=nothing, completion_builder=nothing\n)\n\nBuild the DLLCache for the given model and decomposition.     In this lower-level function (compared to decompose!), users can set     custom projection functions via proj_fn, custom DLL layer builders     via dll_layer_builder and custom completion builders via     completion_builder (same signature, returning (y_pred, p) -> (y, z, w)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.build_cache"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLL.DLLCache","page":"API reference","text":"DLLCache\n\nEverything decompose! builds for a model: the projection y_proj, the DLL layer dll_layer(y_pred, param_value) -> dual objective, the completion(y_pred, param_value) -> (y, z, w) (or nothing when a custom dll_layer_builder is given without a completion_builder), the dual model and the decomposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.DLLCache"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.get_cache","page":"API reference","text":"get_cache(model::JuMP.Model)\n\nGet the DLLCache for the model. Must have called decompose! first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.get_cache"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.dual_objective","page":"API reference","text":"dual_objective(model::JuMP.Model, y_predicted, param_value)\n\nEvaluate the dual objective function (projection and completion).\n\n\n\n\n\ndual_objective(model::JuMP.Model, Y::AbstractMatrix, P::AbstractMatrix) -> Vector\n\nBatched dual_objective: column k of Y is a flattened prediction (see flatten_y) and column k of P the matching parameter values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.dual_objective"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.dual_objective_gradient","page":"API reference","text":"dual_objective_gradient(model::JuMP.Model, y_predicted, param_value; ad_type::DI.AbstractADType=DI.AutoForwardDiff())\n\nEvaluate the gradient of the dual objective function with respect to the predicted dual variables.     This includes both the projection and the completion steps.\n\n\n\n\n\ndual_objective_gradient(model::JuMP.Model, Y::AbstractMatrix, P::AbstractMatrix; ad_type=DI.AutoForwardDiff()) -> Matrix\n\nBatched dual_objective_gradient: column k of the result is the flattened gradient for column k of Y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.dual_objective_gradient"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.dual_objective_and_gradient","page":"API reference","text":"dual_objective_and_gradient(model::JuMP.Model, y_predicted, param_value; ad_type=DI.AutoForwardDiff())\n\nReturn (dual_objective(model, y_predicted, param_value), dual_objective_gradient(model, y_predicted, param_value)) computed in one differentiation pass.\n\n\n\n\n\ndual_objective_and_gradient(model::JuMP.Model, Y::AbstractMatrix, P::AbstractMatrix; ad_type=DI.AutoForwardDiff()) -> (Vector, Matrix)\n\nBatched dual_objective_and_gradient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.dual_objective_and_gradient"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.dual_completion","page":"API reference","text":"dual_completion(model::JuMP.Model, y_predicted, param_value) -> (y, z, w)\n\nProject the predicted y onto its dual cone and complete the remaining dual variables, returning a NamedTuple:\n\ny: projected predictions, one vector per constraint in get_y;\nz: completed dual variables, one vector per constraint in get_z;\nw: quadratic slack variables of the dual (empty for affine objectives).\n\nSigns follow JuMP.dual. By Theorem 1 of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck (2024), when the decomposition's completion is feasible, (y, z, w) is dual feasible, so dual_objective is a valid bound on the optimal value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.dual_completion"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLL.AbstractDecomposition","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractDecomposition\n\nSupertype of DLL decompositions: which primal constraints have their dual variables predicted (y_ref) and which are completed (z). A subtype must have the fields p_ref (parameters) and y_ref (predicted constraints), implement can_decompose for auto-detection, and either have a z_ref field or implement completed_constraints. See the package guide on custom decompositions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.AbstractDecomposition"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLL.BoundDecomposition","page":"API reference","text":"BoundDecomposition(model::JuMP.Model)\n\nCreate a decomposition using z for bound constraints and y for all other constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.BoundDecomposition"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLL.ConvexQP","page":"API reference","text":"ConvexQP(model::JuMP.Model)\n\nCreate a decomposition using z for the quadratic slacks and y for all constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.ConvexQP"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLL.GenericDecomposition","page":"API reference","text":"GenericDecomposition(model::JuMP.Model, y_ref::AbstractVector{<:JuMP.ConstraintRef})\n\nCreate a decomposition using z for all constraints except y_ref.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.GenericDecomposition"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.can_decompose","page":"API reference","text":"can_decompose(model::JuMP.Model, ::Type{<:AbstractDecomposition}) -> Bool\n\nWhether the decomposition type applies to model with a closed-form completion. Used by decompose! to auto-detect, in order: BoundDecomposition, ConvexQP, GenericDecomposition (never auto-detected).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.can_decompose"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.completed_constraints","page":"API reference","text":"completed_constraints(decomposition::AbstractDecomposition) -> Vector{JuMP.ConstraintRef}\n\nPrimal constraints whose dual variables (z) are recovered by the completion step rather than predicted. The fallback is the field decomposition.z_ref.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.completed_constraints"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.get_y","page":"API reference","text":"get_y(model::JuMP.Model)\n\nGet the primal constraints corresponding to the y variables in the decomposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.get_y"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.get_z","page":"API reference","text":"get_z(model::JuMP.Model)\n\nGet the primal constraints whose dual variables (z) are completed rather than predicted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.get_z"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.get_y_dual","page":"API reference","text":"get_y_dual(model::JuMP.Model)\n\nGet the dual variables corresponding to the y variables in the decomposition.     These are VariableRefs belonging to the dual model, not the passed-in model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.get_y_dual"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.y_shape","page":"API reference","text":"y_shape(model::JuMP.Model)\n\nGet the shape of the y variables in the decomposition.     This is a Vector{Int} where each entry is the number of dual variables for that constraint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.y_shape"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.flatten_y","page":"API reference","text":"flatten_y(y)\n\nFlatten a vector of y variables into a single vector, i.e. Vector{Vector{Float64}} -> Vector{Float64}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.flatten_y"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.unflatten_y","page":"API reference","text":"unflatten_y(y::Vector{T}, y_shape::Vector{Int}) where T\n\nUnflatten a vector of flattened y variables into a vector of vectors, i.e. Vector{Float64} -> Vector{Vector{Float64}}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.unflatten_y"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.make_completion_model","page":"API reference","text":"make_completion_model(cache::DLLCache)\n\nCreate a JuMP model for the dual completion step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.make_completion_model"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.bounded_builder","page":"API reference","text":"bounded_builder(decomposition::BoundDecomposition, proj_fn, dual_model; completion=:exact, μ=1.0, backend=nothing)\n\nClosed-form DLL layer (y_pred, param_value) -> dual objective for the bounded decomposition (Example 1 of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck, 2024). See bounded_completion_builder for the completed dual variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.bounded_builder"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.bounded_completion_builder","page":"API reference","text":"bounded_completion_builder(decomposition::BoundDecomposition, proj_fn, dual_model; completion=:exact, μ=1.0, backend=nothing)\n\nClosed-form completion (y_pred, param_value) -> (y, z, w) for the bounded decomposition: y is the projected prediction, z = [[zl_1], …, [zl_n], [zu_1], …, [zu_n]] are the bound duals (in the order of completed_constraints) and w is empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.bounded_completion_builder"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.convex_qp_builder","page":"API reference","text":"convex_qp_builder(decomposition::ConvexQP, proj_fn, dual_model; backend=nothing)\n\nClosed-form DLL layer (y_pred, param_value) -> dual objective for a strictly convex quadratic objective (Example 3 of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck, 2024). See convex_qp_completion_builder for the completed dual variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.convex_qp_builder"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.convex_qp_completion_builder","page":"API reference","text":"convex_qp_completion_builder(decomposition::ConvexQP, proj_fn, dual_model; backend=nothing)\n\nClosed-form completion (y_pred, param_value) -> (y, z, w): y is the projected prediction (all constraints are predicted, so z is empty) and w are the quadratic slack variables of the dual, obtained by solving the dual stationarity equations for w given y. With Dualization's sign convention w equals the minimizer of the Lagrangian, x(y) = Q⁻¹(Aᵀy - c), so w is the primal optimum when y is dual optimal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.convex_qp_completion_builder"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.jump_builder","page":"API reference","text":"jump_builder(decomposition::AbstractDecomposition, proj_fn::Function, dual_model::JuMP.Model, optimizer; silent=true)\n\nBuild the DLL layer (y_pred, param_value) -> dual objective by solving the completion model (problem (9) of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck, 2024) with JuMP.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.jump_builder"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.jump_completion_builder","page":"API reference","text":"jump_completion_builder(decomposition::AbstractDecomposition, proj_fn::Function, dual_model::JuMP.Model, optimizer; silent=true)\n\nBuild the completion (y_pred, param_value) -> (y, z, w) by solving the completion model with JuMP; z follows completed_constraints and w are the quadratic slack variables of the dual (if any).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.jump_completion_builder"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.make_proj_fn","page":"API reference","text":"make_proj_fn(decomposition::AbstractDecomposition, dual_model::JuMP.Model)\n\nCreate a function that projects the raw dual variable predictions onto their dual cone constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.make_proj_fn"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.make_jump_proj_fn","page":"API reference","text":"make_jump_proj_fn(decomposition::AbstractDecomposition, dual_model::JuMP.Model, optimizer; silent=true)\n\nLike make_proj_fn, but computes the Euclidean projection onto the dual cones by solving min ‖y - ŷ‖² with optimizer (for cones without a closed-form projection). Not differentiable with automatic differentiation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.make_jump_proj_fn"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.batched_layer","page":"API reference","text":"batched_layer(model::JuMP.Model) -> Union{AbstractBatchedLayer,Nothing}\n\nThe batched closed-form layer built by decompose! (or nothing for solver-based or custom layers). layer(Y, P) returns the dual objectives of a batch; dual_objective and friends use it automatically for matrix inputs. To run on an accelerator, move it with Adapt.adapt(ArrayType, layer) and pass Y, P of that array type; sparse matrices are converted by the array package's adapt rules (or call densify first).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.batched_layer"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.batched_completion","page":"API reference","text":"batched_completion(layer::AbstractBatchedLayer, Y, P) -> (y, z, w)\n\nBatched dual_completion: matrices with one column per instance. y is the projected prediction, z the completed constraint duals (rows in the order of completed_constraints, i.e. [z_l; z_u] for the bounded decomposition, empty for ConvexQP) and w the quadratic slacks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.batched_completion"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLL.AbstractBatchedLayer","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractBatchedLayer\n\nA closed-form DLL layer evaluated on a whole batch. Call it as layer(Y, P) to get the vector of dual objectives, and use batched_completion for the completed duals. Obtain one with batched_layer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.AbstractBatchedLayer"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLL.BoundedBatchedLayer","page":"API reference","text":"BoundedBatchedLayer\n\nBatched form of bounded_builder: R = A_z [Y; P] .+ b_z (= c - Aᵀy), z_l, z_u by the exact or log-barrier closed form, then the dual objective.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.BoundedBatchedLayer"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLL.ConvexQPBatchedLayer","page":"API reference","text":"ConvexQPBatchedLayer\n\nBatched form of convex_qp_builder: W = Q_d⁻¹ (A_z [Y; P] .+ b_z) (the Lagrangian minimizer), then the dual objective.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.ConvexQPBatchedLayer"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLL.BatchedProjection","page":"API reference","text":"BatchedProjection(decomposition, dual_model; projection = :euclidean)\n\nProjection of a prediction matrix onto the dual cones of the predicted constraints. projection = :euclidean matches make_proj_fn; projection = :radial uses the radial projection of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck (2024, eq. 26) for second-order cones, (t, v) ↦ (max(t, ‖v‖), v) (their eq. 39), which never maps a prediction to zero and so avoids vanishing gradients; for scalar sets both coincide.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.BatchedProjection"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLL.ProjectionBlock","page":"API reference","text":"ProjectionBlock\n\nRows rows of a prediction matrix that belong to one constraint, and how to project them onto the dual cone set: kind is one of :free, :ge, :le, :eq (scalar sets with constant a), :nonneg, :nonpos, :zeros, :soc, :soc_radial or :generic (per-column MathOptSetDistances fallback, CPU only).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.ProjectionBlock"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.densify","page":"API reference","text":"densify(layer::AbstractBatchedLayer) -> AbstractBatchedLayer\n\nCopy of layer with dense matrices (convenient before Adapt.adapt to array types without sparse support).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.densify"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.objective_hessian","page":"API reference","text":"objective_hessian(model::JuMP.Model, x_ref = decision variables) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nHessian of the objective with respect to the decision variables x_ref (terms involving parameters are excluded): for f(x) = ½ xᵀHx + …, returns H.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.objective_hessian"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.is_strictly_convex_objective","page":"API reference","text":"is_strictly_convex_objective(model::JuMP.Model) -> Bool\n\nWhether the objective is strictly convex in the decision variables (for Min; strictly concave for Max), i.e. its Hessian is positive (negative) definite. This is the assumption of the ConvexQP closed-form completion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.is_strictly_convex_objective"},{"category":"function","location":"public/#L2ODLL.DLLMethod","page":"API reference","text":"DLLMethod(; decomposition = nothing, optimizer = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nDual Lagrangian Learning as a method of the LearningToOptimize L2OCore interface (an L2OCore.AbstractDualMethod). Available after import L2OCore (package extension).\n\ndecomposition: nothing to auto-detect (as in decompose!), or a function model -> AbstractDecomposition, e.g. L2ODLL.BoundDecomposition.\noptimizer: used when the decomposition has no closed-form completion.\nkwargs...: forwarded to decompose!, e.g. projection = :radial or completion = :log, μ = 0.1.\n\nimport L2OCore, L2ODLL\npipe = L2OCore.L2OPipeline(L2OCore.ParametricProblem(model), L2ODLL.DLLMethod())\npred = L2OCore.complete(pipe, P, Y)        # completed duals, dual_layout order\nreport = L2OCore.evaluate(pipe, P, pred) # report.dual_bound ≤ optimal values\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLL.DLLMethod"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLLL2OCoreExt.DLLDecomposition","page":"API reference","text":"DLLDecomposition <: L2OCore.AbstractDecomposition\n\nThe L2OCore view of an L2ODLL.DLLCache: the proxy predicts the flattened y (output_dimension = sum(L2ODLL.y_shape(model)) rows) and the completion fills the remaining duals. perm maps the stacked vector [flatten_y(y); flatten_y(z)] to L2OCore's dual_layout order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLLL2OCoreExt.DLLDecomposition"},{"category":"type","location":"public/#L2ODLLL2OCoreExt.DualLagrangianLearning","page":"API reference","text":"DualLagrangianLearning(decomposition, optimizer) <: L2OCore.AbstractDualMethod\n\nDual Lagrangian Learning (Tanneau and Van Hentenryck, 2024). Construct it with L2ODLL.DLLMethod.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2ODLLL2OCoreExt.DualLagrangianLearning"},{"category":"method","location":"public/#L2OCore.complete-Tuple{L2ODLLL2OCoreExt.DualLagrangianLearning, L2OCore.L2OPipeline, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"complete(::DualLagrangianLearning, pipe, inputs, output) -> Prediction\n\nProject each predicted y onto its dual cone and complete the remaining duals (closed form for BoundDecomposition / ConvexQP, a solver otherwise). The returned dual is num_duals × B in dual_layout order with JuMP.dual signs.\n\nBy Theorems 1-2 of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck (2024), the completed point is dual feasible, so its Lagrangian value bounds the optimum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2OCore.complete"},{"category":"method","location":"public/#L2OCore.generate_data-Tuple{L2ODLLL2OCoreExt.DualLagrangianLearning, L2OCore.L2OPipeline}","page":"API reference","text":"generate_data(::DualLagrangianLearning, pipe; sampler, n, rng, optimizer = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nDLL is self-supervised: without optimizer the dataset holds only the sampled inputs. With optimizer, every instance is solved and the optimal objective values and duals are recorded (useful for comparing the dual bounds with the optimal values and reporting gaps).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2OCore.generate_data"},{"category":"method","location":"public/#L2OCore.loss-Tuple{L2ODLLL2OCoreExt.DualLagrangianLearning, L2OCore.L2OPipeline, Any, Any}","page":"API reference","text":"loss(::DualLagrangianLearning, pipe, inputs, output; kwargs...) -> Real\n\nSelf-supervised DLL loss, eq. (27a) of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck (2024): the mean dual objective of the completed predictions, negated for minimization problems so that the loss is always minimized. Needs no labels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"L2OCore.loss"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/markowitz/#Markowitz-portfolio-(second-order-cone)","page":"Markowitz portfolio (SOCP)","text":"A risk-constrained portfolio problem: maximize expected return subject to a budget and a second-order cone bound on the standard deviation,\n\nmax_x mu^top x quad textstquad mathbf1^top x = 1\n(gamma L^top x) in mathcalQ 0 le x le 1\n\nwith the expected returns mu and the risk budget gamma as parameters (Sigma = L L^top). Every variable is bounded and the objective is linear, so L2ODLL uses the BoundDecomposition (Example 1 of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck, 2024): the proxy predicts the duals of the budget and of the cone, and the bound duals are completed in closed form.\n\nusing JuMP, LinearAlgebra, Random\nusing Clarabel: Clarabel\nusing L2ODLL\n\nΣ = [166 34 58; 34 64 4; 58 4 100] / 100^2\nN = size(Σ, 1)\n\nmodel = Model()\n@variable(model, 0 <= x[1:N] <= 1)\n@variable(model, μ[1:N] in MOI.Parameter.(0.0))\n@variable(model, γ in MOI.Parameter(0.1))\n@constraint(model, budget, sum(x) == 1)\n@constraint(model, risk, [γ; cholesky(Σ).L * x] in SecondOrderCone())\n@objective(model, Max, dot(μ, x))\n\ncache = decompose!(model)\ncache.decomposition\nBoundDecomposition(JuMP.VariableRef[μ[1], μ[2], μ[3], γ], JuMP.ConstraintRef[budget : x[1] + x[2] + x[3] = 1, risk : [γ, 0.12884098726725127 x[1], 0.02638911787401531 x[1] + 0.07552227789090664 x[2], 0.0450167304909673 x[1] - 0.01043336918898456 x[2] + 0.08868279868875341 x[3]] ∈ MathOptInterface.SecondOrderCone(4)], JuMP.ConstraintRef[x[1] ≥ 0, x[2] ≥ 0, x[3] ≥ 0], JuMP.ConstraintRef[x[1] ≤ 1, x[2] ≤ 1, x[3] ≤ 1])\n\nThe predicted dual variables: one for the budget and four for the cone.\n\nget_y(model), y_shape(model)\n(JuMP.ConstraintRef[budget : x[1] + x[2] + x[3] = 1, risk : [γ, 0.12884098726725127 x[1], 0.02638911787401531 x[1] + 0.07552227789090664 x[2], 0.0450167304909673 x[1] - 0.01043336918898456 x[2] + 0.08868279868875341 x[3]] ∈ MathOptInterface.SecondOrderCone(4)], [1, 4])","title":"Markowitz portfolio (second-order cone)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/markowitz/#Any-prediction-gives-a-valid-bound","page":"Markowitz portfolio (SOCP)","text":"For a maximization problem the dual objective is an upper bound. We draw parameters, solve the problem for reference, and evaluate random predictions.\n\nrng = Xoshiro(42)\npv = [0.1 .* randn(rng, N); 0.1]\nset_parameter_value.([μ; γ], pv)\nset_optimizer(model, Clarabel.Optimizer);\nset_silent(model);\noptimize!(model)\nopt = objective_value(model)\n\nbounds = [dual_objective(model, [randn(rng, n) for n in y_shape(model)], pv) for _ in 1:5]\nall(bounds .>= opt - 1e-8)\ntrue\n\nThe optimal dual solution recovers the optimum (strong duality):\n\nystar = dual.(get_y(model))\ndual_objective(model, ystar, pv), opt\n(0.024115869898563985, 0.024115869198883387)\n\nThe completed dual variables are available as well:\n\ndual_completion(model, ystar, pv).z\n6-element Vector{Vector{Float64}}:\n [0.0]\n [4.6491517580182773e-10]\n [5.424060103842443e-9]\n [-3.4893377076627985e-10]\n [-0.0]\n [-0.0]","title":"Any prediction gives a valid bound"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/markowitz/#Gradients-and-batches","page":"Markowitz portfolio (SOCP)","text":"The gradient with respect to the prediction (ForwardDiff by default) and the matrix forms, with the batch as the last dimension:\n\nB = 8\nP = reduce(hcat, [[0.1 .* randn(rng, N); 0.05 + 0.1rand(rng)] for _ in 1:B])\nY = randn(rng, sum(y_shape(model)), B)\nvals, G = dual_objective_and_gradient(model, Y, P)\nsize(G)\n(5, 8)\n\n","title":"Gradients and batches"},{"category":"section","location":"#L2ODLL.jl","page":"Home","text":"L2ODLL.jl implements Dual Lagrangian Learning (DLL) of Tanneau and Van Hentenryck (2024) for parametric conic and convex quadratic problems modeled in JuMP, with the bounded-variable completions of Klamkin, Tanneau and Van Hentenryck.\n\nA neural network (or any proxy) predicts some of the dual variables hat y of a primal problem from its parameters p. L2ODLL projects the prediction onto the dual cone and completes the remaining dual variables so that the result is dual feasible. Its dual objective is therefore a valid bound on the optimal value for every prediction (weak duality), and it is differentiable in hat y, so it is also the self-supervised training loss: maximizing it needs no optimal solutions.\n\nflowchart LR\n    p[\"parameters p\"] --> f[\"proxy f(p)\"]\n    f -->|\"ŷ\"| proj[\"Π onto dual cone\"]\n    proj -->|\"y\"| comp[\"dual completion z(y, p)\"]\n    comp --> bound[\"Φ(y, p) ≤ OPT(p)\"]\n    bound -.->|\"∇ŷ Φ (training)\"| f\n\nL2ODLL takes the primal JuMP model, dualizes it with Dualization.jl, detects a decomposition with a closed-form completion and builds the projection, completion, bound and gradient functions automatically.","title":"L2ODLL.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"L2ODLL is not registered yet. Its optional L2OCore interface (L2ODLL.DLLMethod) comes from the development version of LearningToOptimize.jl; the registered LearningToOptimize 1.0.0 predates it. Install the core subpackage first, then L2ODLL, on any Julia version from 1.10:\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.add(; url = \"https://github.com/LearningToOptimize/LearningToOptimize.jl\", subdir = \"lib/L2OCore\")\nPkg.add(; url = \"https://github.com/LearningToOptimize/L2ODLL.jl\")\nPkg.add(\"JuMP\")   # used by every example\n\nThe examples also use HiGHS and Clarabel: Pkg.add([\"HiGHS\", \"Clarabel\"]).","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Example","page":"Home","text":"using JuMP, L2ODLL\n\nmodel = Model()\n@variable(model, 0 <= x[1:2] <= 1)\n@variable(model, d in MOI.Parameter(1.0))          # parameter: the demand\n@constraint(model, balance, x[1] + x[2] == d)\n@objective(model, Min, x[1] + 3x[2])\n\ndecompose!(model)                                   # detects BoundDecomposition\n\ndual_objective(model, [[2.0]], [1.5])               # 2.0 ≤ OPT(1.5) = 2.5, for any prediction\ndual_objective(model, [[3.0]], [1.5])               # 2.5: the optimal price gives the optimum\ndual_objective_gradient(model, [[2.0]], [1.5])      # training signal\n\nY = randn(sum(y_shape(model)), 64)                  # batch: predictions × instances\nP = 1 .+ rand(1, 64)                                # parameters × instances\nbounds, G = dual_objective_and_gradient(model, Y, P)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"Theory: the optimization problems, the completions in closed form and their gradients, with equation numbers of the papers.\nPackage guide: getting started, concepts, custom decompositions, projections and layers.\nL2OCore interface: using L2ODLL as a method of the LearningToOptimize pipeline; AD and GPU notes.\nExamples: Markowitz portfolio, economic dispatch (training a proxy).","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-LearningToOptimize-ecosystem","page":"Home","text":"Package What it provides\nLearningToOptimize.jl the penalty method, Flux training and solver-labeled dataset generation; hosts the two subpackages below\n↳ L2OCore the shared interface: method families, problem wrapper, batched evaluators, datasets\n↳ L2OSobolev derivative (Sobolev) supervision added to any method\nL2ODLL.jl Dual Lagrangian Learning: dual proxies that give valid dual bounds for conic problems\nL2ODC3.jl DC3: equality completion and inequality correction for primal proxies\nL2OALM.jl primal-dual learning with an augmented Lagrangian\n\n","title":"The LearningToOptimize ecosystem"}]
}
